+# Reproducible single-core benchmark
+
+The primary tracked workloads measure complete output generation. They never
+replace the generator with a tape or checksum-only result. Each timed API call
+is followed by a compiler memory barrier; output bytes, `lengths`, RNG state,
+and `call_count` are checksummed after timing. Two separately named component
+controls are available for cost modeling only and cannot count toward the
+generator throughput target.
+
+Reportable runs use at least nine independent samples and print median, P5, and
+P95 throughput plus median/P95 latency. `CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW` provides wall
+time. Serialized TSC readings are explicitly reported as reference cycles, not
+actual core cycles. The harness also tries Linux `perf_event_open` for actual
+core cycles, instructions, branches, branch misses, and cache misses. WSL may
+deny or omit those counters; an unavailable result is printed rather than
+silently substituting TSC cycles.
+
+## Workloads
+
+- `scalar-generate-l1`: one context and one cache-resident 64-byte buffer.
+- `scalar-variant-l1`: the corresponding dedicated-fast-path-disabled control.
+- `batch-l1`: AVX2 batches of 64 across an exact 24 KiB output/length ring.
+- `batch-l2`: AVX2 batches of 64 across an approximately 255 KiB ring.
+- `batch-dram`: AVX2 batches of 1024 written sequentially over a 256 MiB
+  allocation by default. The allocation is not repeatedly cleared in timing.
+- `control-rng-l1`: non-generator control containing the exact two four-lane
+  xoroshiro128++ advances required per four expressions. It stores final RNG
+  state and a checksum-visible accumulator but does not materialize output.
+- `control-store-l1`: non-generator control that really writes 32 bytes,
+  a newline, and a four-byte length per record into an 18 KiB L1 ring. It uses
+  a fixed record and performs no RNG or classification.
+
+The effective GB/s field uses an untimed one-million-expression sample of
+contract bytes written (including batch newlines). It is not used to keep the
+timed loop alive; the post-timing checksum serves that purpose.
+
+## Commands
+
+From WSL, first run the correctness gate:
+
+```bash
+bash tests/run-tests.sh
+bash tests/run-sanitizers.sh
+```
+
+Run AVX2 batch cases on an identified P-core (CPU 0 is only an example):
+
+```bash
+MODE=avx2 bash benchmarks/run-benchmark.sh --cpu 0 \
+  --case batch-l1 --case batch-l2 --case batch-dram
+```
+
+Run the true scalar build separately:
+
+```bash
+MODE=scalar bash benchmarks/run-benchmark.sh --cpu 0 \
+  --case scalar-generate-l1 --case scalar-variant-l1
+```
+
+Use `CC=clang` for the compiler comparison. `--quick` produces a short,
+explicitly non-reportable diagnostic run. Full result capture should preserve
+all `ENV`, `META`, `RUN`, `SUMMARY`, and `FINAL` lines.
+
+`EXTRA_CFLAGS` appends explicitly recorded compiler options without changing
+the default. For example:
+
+```bash
+EXTRA_CFLAGS=-fomit-frame-pointer MODE=avx2 \
+  bash benchmarks/run-benchmark.sh --quick --cpu 10 --case batch-l1
+```
+
+The process pins itself with `sched_setaffinity` and verifies the observed CPU.
+On hybrid CPUs it also reports CPUID leaf `0x1a` core type when the hypervisor
+exposes it. If WSL does not expose hybrid topology, identify the fast P-core set
+empirically and record that limitation with the result.
+
+On Windows, list logical processor efficiency classes with:
+
+```powershell
+./benchmarks/windows-cpu-topology.ps1 | Format-Table -AutoSize
+```
+
+The following wrapper runs the nine-sample suite and samples Windows `Processor
+Information` frequency/performance-limit counters for the selected logical CPU:
+
+```powershell
+./benchmarks/run-reportable.ps1 -Mode avx2 -Cpu 10
+./benchmarks/run-reportable.ps1 -Mode scalar -Cpu 10
+./benchmarks/run-reportable.ps1 -Mode avx2 -Cpu 10 `
+  -Cases @('control-rng-l1','control-store-l1')
+```
+
+Pass `-Compiler clang` for compiler comparison, `-ExtraCFlags` for recorded
+option experiments, or `-Cases` for an explicit workload list. The wrapper
+creates a raw benchmark text file and a timestamped telemetry CSV in
+`benchmarks/results/`. Windows logical CPU numbering and WSL vCPU numbering are
+recorded as an assumption when the hypervisor does not expose an explicit map.
